Specification ImportOfx

Description Reading a QFX or OFX download, and deciding for each transaction in
# it whether it is new, already present, or the arrival of something
# entered by hand.
Details
# Importing the same file twice must change nothing. That is the single
# requirement everything in this file serves, because over twenty years
# of downloads it is certain to happen by accident.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the file says
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity OfxStatement
Description The header of one statement in the file, naming the account it is for.
| Attribute    | Type        | Default    | Notes                             |
| BankId       | Text        | none       | Routing number; absent on cards   |
| OfxAccountId | Text        |            | The account number the bank uses  |
| OfxAcctType  | OfxAcctType | CHECKING   |                                   |
| Currency     | Text        | USD        |                                   |
| StartDate    | Date        | 2024-01-01 | Of the period downloaded          |
| EndDate      | Date        | 2024-01-31 |                                   |
| LedgerBal    | Money       | 0.00       | What the bank says the balance is |
| LedgerBalAt  | Date        | 2024-01-31 |                                   |

DataType OfxAcctType
Description The account type OFX reports, which is not the SimpleAccount type.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value       | Notes                                   |
| CHECKING    |                                         |
| SAVINGS     |                                         |
| MONEYMRKT   |                                         |
| CREDITLINE  |                                         |
| CREDITCARD  | From a CCACCTFROM block rather than BANKACCTFROM |
| INVESTMENT  | From an INVACCTFROM block               |

Entity OfxTransaction
Description One STMTTRN block.
| Attribute  | Type        | Default    | Notes                              |
| TrnType    | OfxTrnType  | DEBIT      |                                    |
| DatePosted | Date        | 2024-01-01 | From DTPOSTED                      |
| Amount     | Money       | 0.00       | From TRNAMT, signed by the bank    |
| FitId      | FitId       |            | From FITID                         |
| Name       | Text        | none       | From NAME, the payee as the bank has it |
| Memo       | Text        | none       | From MEMO                          |
| CheckNum   | CheckNumber | none       | From CHECKNUM                      |

DataType OfxTrnType
Description The transaction type OFX reports. It is recorded but not relied on:
# the sign of the amount is what decides direction.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                       |
| CREDIT    | Money in                    |
| DEBIT     | Money out                   |
| INT       | Interest earned or charged  |
| DIV       | Dividend                    |
| FEE       | Service charge              |
| SRVCHG    | Service charge              |
| DEP       | Deposit                     |
| ATM       |                             |
| POS       | Point of sale               |
| XFER      | Transfer                    |
| CHECK     | A paper check               |
| PAYMENT   |                             |
| CASH      |                             |
| DIRECTDEP | Direct deposit              |
| DIRECTDEBIT |                           |
| REPEATPMT | A standing order            |
| OTHER     |                             |

Collection OfxTransactions
| DataType       | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| OfxTransaction | 0       | 100000  | FitId is unique per statement |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Parsing the file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Reading an OFX 1 file, which is SGML and not XML
Description Most bank downloads are OFX 1.0.2 with unclosed tags, and a strict
# XML parser rejects them. The reader closes a tag when the next one
# opens at the same level, which is what the format intends.
Given the file contains
"""
OFXHEADER:100
DATA:OFXSGML
VERSION:102
SECURITY:NONE
ENCODING:USASCII

<OFX>
<BANKMSGSRSV1><STMTTRNRS><STMTRS>
<CURDEF>USD
<BANKACCTFROM><BANKID>053000196<ACCTID>1234567890<ACCTTYPE>CHECKING</BANKACCTFROM>
<BANKTRANLIST><DTSTART>20240101<DTEND>20240131
<STMTTRN><TRNTYPE>DEBIT<DTPOSTED>20240115120000[-5:EST]<TRNAMT>-45.00<FITID>20240115001<NAME>HARRIS TEETER #231<MEMO>PURCHASE</STMTTRN>
<STMTTRN><TRNTYPE>CHECK<DTPOSTED>20240118<TRNAMT>-1250.00<FITID>20240118002<CHECKNUM>2041<NAME>COUNTY TAX OFFICE</STMTTRN>
<STMTTRN><TRNTYPE>DIRECTDEP<DTPOSTED>20240131<TRNAMT>3000.00<FITID>20240131003<NAME>ACME PAYROLL</STMTTRN>
</BANKTRANLIST>
<LEDGERBAL><BALAMT>1705.00<DTASOF>20240131</LEDGERBAL>
</STMTRS></STMTTRNRS></BANKMSGSRSV1>
</OFX>
"""
Then statement is : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
| Currency     | USD        |
| StartDate    | 2024-01-01 |
| EndDate      | 2024-01-31 |
| LedgerBal    | 1705.00    |
| LedgerBalAt  | 2024-01-31 |
Then transactions read are : OfxTransactions
| TrnType   | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId       | Name               | Memo     | CheckNum |
| DEBIT     | 2024-01-15 | -45.00   | 20240115001 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | PURCHASE | none     |
| CHECK     | 2024-01-18 | -1250.00 | 20240118002 | COUNTY TAX OFFICE  | none     | 2041     |
| DIRECTDEP | 2024-01-31 | 3000.00  | 20240131003 | ACME PAYROLL       | none     | none     |

Scenario Reading an OFX 2 file, which is XML
Description Newer downloads are well-formed XML with every tag closed. The same
# reader handles both, so nothing downstream knows which it was.
Given the file contains
"""
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<?OFX OFXHEADER="200" VERSION="211" SECURITY="NONE"?>
<OFX>
  <CREDITCARDMSGSRSV1><CCSTMTTRNRS><CCSTMTRS>
    <CURDEF>USD</CURDEF>
    <CCACCTFROM><ACCTID>4111111111111111</ACCTID></CCACCTFROM>
    <BANKTRANLIST>
      <DTSTART>20240301</DTSTART><DTEND>20240331</DTEND>
      <STMTTRN>
        <TRNTYPE>DEBIT</TRNTYPE><DTPOSTED>20240305</DTPOSTED>
        <TRNAMT>-89.99</TRNAMT><FITID>CC-20240305-1</FITID>
        <NAME>AMAZON MKTPLACE</NAME>
      </STMTTRN>
    </BANKTRANLIST>
    <LEDGERBAL><BALAMT>-89.99</BALAMT><DTASOF>20240331</DTASOF></LEDGERBAL>
  </CCSTMTRS></CCSTMTTRNRS></CREDITCARDMSGSRSV1>
</OFX>
"""
Then statement is : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | none             |
| OfxAccountId | 4111111111111111 |
| OfxAcctType  | CREDITCARD       |
| Currency     | USD              |
| StartDate    | 2024-03-01       |
| EndDate      | 2024-03-31       |
| LedgerBal    | -89.99           |
| LedgerBalAt  | 2024-03-31       |
Then transactions read are : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId         | Name            | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-03-05 | -89.99 | CC-20240305-1 | AMAZON MKTPLACE | none | none     |

BusinessRule An OFX date is read to the day
Description DTPOSTED may carry a time and a timezone. The time is discarded:
# a register is kept by date, and keeping the time would make the
# same transaction sort differently depending on the reader timezone.
Examples: OfxDateParse
| Text                      | Date       | Notes                           |
| 20240115                  | 2024-01-15 | Date only                       |
| 20240115120000            | 2024-01-15 | Time discarded                  |
| 20240115120000.000        | 2024-01-15 | Milliseconds discarded          |
| 20240115120000[-5:EST]    | 2024-01-15 | Timezone discarded              |
| 20240115235959[-5:EST]    | 2024-01-15 | Not rolled into the next day    |
| 20240101000000[+13:NZDT]  | 2024-01-01 | Nor into the previous one       |

Attributes OfxDateParse
| Attribute | Type   | Default    |
| Text      | String |            |
| Date      | Date   | 2024-01-01 |
| Notes     | String | none       |

BusinessRule The amount in an OFX file is used as the transaction amount
Description TRNAMT is signed from the point of view of the account the statement
# is for, which is the same convention the transactions use. No account
# type needs a sign flip, including a credit card: a charge is
# negative in the file and a credit to the card in the book.
Examples: OfxAmountToPosting
| OfxAcctType | TrnAmt  | PostingAmount | Notes                        |
| CHECKING    | -45.00  | -45.00        | Money out of checking        |
| CHECKING    | 3000.00 | 3000.00       | Pay in                       |
| CREDITCARD  | -89.99  | -89.99        | A charge; more is now owed   |
| CREDITCARD  | 300.00  | 300.00        | A payment; less is now owed  |
| SAVINGS     | 1.23    | 1.23          | Interest                     |

Attributes OfxAmountToPosting
| Attribute     | Type        | Default  |
| OfxAcctType   | OfxAcctType | CHECKING |
| TrnAmt        | Money       | 0.00     |
| PostingAmount | Money       | 0.00     |
| Notes         | String      | none     |

Scenario A file that is not OFX at all is reported, not guessed at
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount
2024-01-15,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00
"""
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| No OFX header found; this looks like a CSV file         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which account a download is for
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A download names its account in the bank's own terms -- a routing
# number, and the number the bank keeps the account under -- and the
# account itself is where those terms are kept. The account a
# statement belongs to is the account whose number the statement
# names.
Details There is no table of mappings. An account carries the name of its
# bank, that bank's id and its own number, the three of them together;
# see what is kept about an account in Accounts. Finding the account
# for a download is reading those off the chart. A second place to
# record a bank's numbering would be a second thing to keep in step
# with the first, and the only question a download asks -- which
# account is this -- is answered by an account or not at all.
#
# A number is kept as a person writes it and a file gives bare digits,
# so it is the digits of each that are compared; the rule below says
# how. The routing numbers have to agree whenever both say anything,
# because two banks number their accounts independently and over
# twenty years one of them reaches a number the other has used.
#
# An account that knows none of this is matched by nothing, and that is
# the ordinary state of every account brought over from Quicken: a QIF
# carries no routing number and no account number. The first download
# into such an account is where they arrive; see what a download
# teaches an account, below.

$TBD
Scenario A statement whose number is on no account asks which account it is
Description The first download from an institution names a number that means
# nothing yet. The import stops and asks, rather than inventing an
# account.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | BankId | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | none   | none          |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
Then asked to choose an account for : UnknownAccount Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
| Suggestion   | none       |

Attributes UnknownAccount
Description What the dialog is told about a statement no account claims.
| Attribute    | Type        | Default  |
| BankId       | Text        | none     |
| OfxAccountId | Text        |          |
| OfxAcctType  | OfxAcctType | CHECKING |
| Suggestion   | AccountPath | none     |

$TBD
Scenario A download whose number is on an account imports without asking
Description Which is every month after the first. The account was told the
# bank's numbers once and nobody is asked again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | BankId    | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | 053000196 | 1234567890    |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
Then import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
| Asked       | false           |

Attributes ImportTarget
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| AccountPath | AccountPath |         |
| Asked       | Boolean     | false   |

$TBD
BusinessRule When the number in a download is the number on an account
Description Asked of every real account in the chart. One account saying yes is
# the account it goes to; none, and the import asks. Two is also
# asked, because a number that names two accounts has named neither.
Details Only the digits of each are compared, because the account's number
# is kept as somebody wrote it -- 0012-3456, or a card number in
# groups of four -- and the file gives nothing but digits.
#
# Equal digits are the ordinary case. Beyond that, the digits of one
# ending the digits of the other count, which is what lets an account
# labelled with the last four of a card meet the full number the
# download carries. Four digits is the floor, the same floor the
# file-name rule uses in UserInterface: three digits is a month or a
# sequence number, and matching an account on three digits would put
# a statement in the wrong account for a living.
#
# The routing numbers have to agree whenever both are stated. A card
# statement states none, and an account that has not been told its
# bank's id states none; neither of those is a disagreement, because
# nothing was claimed.
Examples: DownloadAccountMatch
| StatementBankId | StatementAccountId | AccountBankId | AccountNumber        | Matches |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 053000196     | 1234567890           | true    |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | none          | 1234567890           | true    |
| none            | 4111111111114455   | 053000196     | ~4111 1111 1111 4455 | true    |
| 053000196       | 0012-3456          | 053000196     | 0012 3456            | true    |
| 053000196       | 4111111111114455   | 053000196     | 4455                 | true    |
| 053000196       | 4111111111114455   | 053000196     | 455                  | false   |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 053000196     | 0012-3456            | false   |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 021000021     | 1234567890           | false   |
| 053000196       | 1234567890         | 053000196     | none                 | false   |
# The third row is a card: no routing number in the file, one on the account,
# and the number written in groups of four. The second to last is the one the
# routing number is there for -- the same account number at two banks.

Attributes DownloadAccountMatch
| Attribute          | Type    | Default |
| StatementBankId    | Text    | none    |
| StatementAccountId | Text    |         |
| AccountBankId      | Text    | none    |
| AccountNumber      | Text    | none    |
| Matches            | Boolean | false   |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a download teaches an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description An account that was never told the bank's numbers is told them by
# the first download confirmed for it. Nothing already there is
# changed.
Details This is the whole of how the numbers get onto an account without
# anybody typing them. A history imported from Quicken carries none,
# so asking once per account is the least that can be asked, and
# writing down the answer is what makes it once rather than monthly.
#
# Only what is blank is filled in. A number a person typed is what the
# account dialog shows them, and a download that disagreed with it
# would be rewriting a label they chose, in a place they were not
# looking, on the strength of their having confirmed something else.
# So a disagreement leaves the account alone and writes a note, and
# the account dialog is where it is settled.

$TBD
