Description The number was typed in the account dialog and the routing number
# was not, because nobody reads a routing number off a statement to
# label an account. The download has one, the account has none, and
# none is not a disagreement.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | BankId | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       | none   | 1234567890    |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       | none   | none          |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1234567890 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
Then import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
| Asked       | false           |
Then account numbering is : AccountNumbering
| Path            | BankId    | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking | 053000196 | 1234567890    |
# Nobody was asked -- the number was enough to place it -- and the routing
# number was still learned, so a second account at a second bank with the
# same number can be told from this one later.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deciding what to do with each transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType ImportDisposition
Description What the import decided about one downloaded transaction.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                                                       |
| New       | Not seen before; a transaction is created                   |
| Duplicate | Same FITID already imported into this account; nothing done  |
| Matched   | Matches one entered by hand; that one is cleared instead    |
| Possible  | Looks like one already present, but not certainly; asks     |

BusinessRule An identifier is only an identifier to the file that gave it
Description A CSV and a QFX describing the same transaction do not agree on what
# to call it. So an identifier is stored with the name of the source
# that gave it, and the identifier test applies only where the source
# matches the import being done. Against any other source the transaction
# counts as having no identifier at all, and the weaker tests decide.
Details Without this, importing a QFX after a CSV finds no FITID anywhere and
# calls the whole statement new, duplicating a month of transactions.
# With a single identifier field instead of one per source it is no
# better the other way round: stamping the FITID over the CSV reference
# would make the next CSV import offer every row again.
#
# So a transaction collects identifiers rather than having one. A transaction
# imported from a CSV and later recognised in a QFX carries both, and
# re-importing either file does nothing.
Examples: IdentifierScope
| PostingCarries        | ImportIs | TestOneApplies | Then                                 |
| Nothing               | Ofx      | No               | The weaker tests decide            |
| An Ofx id, the same   | Ofx      | Yes              | Duplicate                          |
| An Ofx id, a different one | Ofx | No               | The weaker tests decide            |
| A Csv id only         | Ofx      | No               | As if it had none; may match       |
| A Csv id only         | Csv      | Yes, if it is the same | Duplicate                    |
| A Csv id and an Ofx id | Ofx     | Yes, on the Ofx one | Duplicate                       |

Attributes IdentifierScope
| Attribute      | Type   | Default |
| PostingCarries | String |         |
| ImportIs       | String |         |
| TestOneApplies | String |         |
| Then           | String |         |

BusinessRule How a downloaded transaction is identified
Description In order. The first test that applies decides.
Details The FITID test is what makes a second import of the same file do
# nothing. It is scoped to the account, because two banks may issue the
# same identifier.
#
# Below it the tests divide by what the book's transaction is, and
# cleared status is what says which: everything an import writes is
# cleared, so an uncleared transaction is one somebody typed. See every
# imported transaction is cleared, below.
#
# A transaction somebody typed carries their date, which is the day they
# wrote the check or made the payment, and the bank posts it days later.
# Where there is a check number the number does the identifying and the
# lag is simply allowed for, which is what the check-number window is.
# Where there is not, amount and date are the whole of the evidence, and
# an import that reached across several days on that much would be
# guessing. So test three is dated to the day as well, and what it
# produces is a question rather than a decision: the same amount on the
# same day as something typed is offered as a possible duplicate, and
# the user says whether it is the same payment.
#
# A transaction an import wrote carries the bank's own posting date, and
# so does the row now being read. Two records of one event from one
# source agree about the day, so test four needs no window and asks
# nothing: the same amount in the same account on the same date is that
# transaction arriving a second time. Allowing days either side would be
# allowing the bank to disagree with itself, and asking about it would
# be asking the user to confirm what the program already knows.
#
# Reaching further than a day is left to a pass of its own, after the
# import has been accepted, where the question is a different one:
# pairing a transaction in this register against one in another
# register, the two halves of a transfer days apart. That pass has a
# window because the two dates come from two different places. Nothing
# in it happens while a row is being judged. See proposing transfers.
#
# Tests two to four have no identifier behind them, so they are subject to
# the claim-pairing rule in ImportCsv: each downloaded transaction claims
# at most one of those already there, and a claimed one is not available
# to claim again. Test one needs none of that, because a FITID is unique
# within the account and cannot be claimed twice.
Examples: DispositionRule
| Test | Condition                                                             | Disposition |
| 1    | A transaction in this account carries this FITID, from an Ofx import   | Duplicate   |
| 2    | An uncleared transaction in this account has the same amount and check number, within the book's check-number window | Matched |
| 3    | An uncleared transaction in this account has the same amount on the same date | Possible |
| 4    | A cleared transaction in this account has the same amount on the same date, and no FITID | Matched |
| 5    | Nothing above applies                                                 | New         |

Attributes DispositionRule
| Attribute   | Type              | Default |
| Test        | Integer           | 0       |
| Condition   | String            |         |
| Disposition | ImportDisposition | New     |

