$TBD
BusinessRule A download arrives cleared and only a typed transaction is not
Description Cleared means the bank has said this happened. A download is the
# bank saying so, so everything read out of one is cleared. A
# transaction is uncleared only while the only record of it is that
# somebody typed it.
Details Which makes cleared status the one thing that says where a
# transaction came from, and the tests above lean on it: an uncleared
# transaction is a typed one, so its date is a person's and is allowed
# to differ from the bank's by a few days. A cleared one came from the
# bank, so its date is the bank's and has to agree exactly. Neither
# test would be safe if a download left anything uncleared.
#
# A download states a cleared status of its own in some formats, and it
# is not read. Nothing a bank has sent is uncleared by the time it is
# in the file, so a flag saying otherwise is either about something
# else or wrong.
#
# A QIF history is the exception, and it is not a download: it is one
# migration of twenty years of a Quicken file, and the C field in it is
# the only thing that says which of those invoices were ever paid.
# Three income figures agree with Quicken to the cent because of it.
# So a history keeps the marker it carries, which leaves the
# transactions it brought in cleared where Quicken had cleared them and
# uncleared where it had not. See the cleared marker rule in ImportQif,
# and was_paid in qif_import.h for what that marker replaced.
Examples: ClearedOnArrival
| HowItArrived              | Cleared          |
| Read from an OFX download | Cleared          |
| Read from a CSV download  | Cleared          |
| Read from a QIF history   | As the file says |
| Typed into the register   | Uncleared        |
| Typed as a split          | Uncleared        |

Attributes ClearedOnArrival
| Attribute     | Type   | Default |
| HowItArrived  | String |         |
| Cleared       | String |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a check number is worth, and for how long
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A check number is trusted for a day and no longer
Description A check number is the strongest thing in a bank file: it is written
# on the check itself, so the book and the download are naming the
# same piece of paper. But only for as long as the number means what
# it says. Check numbers are reused -- a new book of checks starts
# where an old one left off, and over twenty years the same number
# comes round again.
Details So a number settles a match only when the two are dated within the
# book's check-number window of each other. Beyond that the number is
# treated as coincidence and the weaker tests decide, which usually means
# the transaction is created.
#
# The window is a setting on the book, a whole number of days, and it is
# one day unless it is changed. One day is right for a book whose checks
# are never entered before the bank reports them: nothing is outstanding,
# so there is nothing for a number to reach back to, and a number that
# matches anything older is more likely a number reused than a check
# still in the post.
#
# This is the only window inside an import. Every test below the number
# asks about one day, so the number is the one thing that reaches further
# than the day the bank posted, and at one day it reaches exactly one day
# further. A book that records checks when they are written has checks
# outstanding for a week or three, and widening the window is what makes
# the number worth having. Recording a check when it is written is not a
# thing this program does yet.
#
# What happens then is the point of the rule. A number that matched while
# the dates did not is exactly the case worth seeing: either a check
# really did take a fortnight to clear, in which case the book now holds
# two of it and one should be deleted, or the number came round again
# and the import was right to create one. The program cannot tell those
# apart, so it does not try: it writes a note against the import and
# shows the notes when the import finishes.
#
# Nothing is silently reconciled on a number alone, and nothing is
# silently left out. A note that nobody reads costs a line on a screen;
# a check quietly matched to the wrong year costs an afternoon with a
# statement.
# Every row here is at the default window of one day.
Examples: CheckNumberWindow
| DaysApart | NumberMatches | Matched | NoteWritten | Notes                                              |
| 0         | Yes           | Yes     | No          | The number settles it                              |
| 1         | Yes           | Yes     | No          | Still within the day                               |
| 2         | Yes           | No      | Yes         | The number is stale and nothing weaker reaches     |
| 16        | Yes           | No      | Yes         | Nothing reaches; created, and the note says why    |
| 16        | No            | No      | No          | A different number, and nothing else reaches       |

Attributes CheckNumberWindow
Description Whether a check number settles a match, by how far apart the two are
# dated, and whether the import wrote a note about it.
Details Matched is the disposition, not the reason for it. The row two days
# apart is created rather than matched, because at the default window
# nothing reaches two days: the number is past its day and the amount
# never reached beyond the day at all. The note is written all the same,
# because the number said one check and the dates said another and that
# is what a person needs to see -- a created transaction is the right
# answer and still worth being told about.
| Attribute     | Type    | Default |
| DaysApart     | Integer | 0       |
| NumberMatches | Text    | Yes     |
| Matched       | Text    | No      |
| NoteWritten   | Text    | No      |
| Notes         | String  | none    |

Scenario A check number that matches too long afterwards is not matched
Description The same number sixteen days later. The number alone is not enough
# to reconcile a check across that gap, so the transaction is created
# and a note says what was seen -- leaving a person to decide whether
# it is the same check or the number come round again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Rates    | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
