# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolving what the import was not sure about
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A possible duplicate is the only disposition that waits
Description The other three settle themselves. A new transaction is created, a
# duplicate is ignored, a match clears the one already there. A
# possible duplicate is the case the program cannot settle, so it is
# the one that asks, and nothing happens to it until it is answered.
Details Only one test produces it, and it is the test about a transaction
# somebody typed: the same amount in the same account on the same day,
# with no check number to settle it. Everything the bank has already
# told us about is settled without asking, because the dates come from
# the bank both times. This is the one case where the two dates come
# from two different hands.
#
# There are two answers and both are ordinary. Either it is the same
# transaction, in which case the one already in the book is the
# transaction and the downloaded row only tells us the bank has seen it;
# or it is a second one, in which case the bank is describing something
# the book does not have.
#
# An import left unanswered changes nothing, which is what makes it safe
# to put down and come back to. A possible duplicate quietly created
# would double a transaction and one quietly discarded would lose it.
# Over twenty years both happen, and the second is worse: an extra row is
# seen and deleted, a missing one is found a year later or never.
Examples: PossibleAnswer
| Answer     | TheOneAlreadyThere                       | TheDownloadedRow            |
| TheSame    | Cleared, and stamped with the identifier | Nothing is created          |
| Different  | Nothing                                  | A transaction is created    |
| Unanswered | Nothing                                  | Nothing; it is asked again  |

Attributes PossibleAnswer
| Attribute          | Type   | Default |
| Answer             | String |         |
| TheOneAlreadyThere | String |         |
| TheDownloadedRow   | String |         |

Scenario Answering that a possible duplicate is the same transaction
Description The one already in the book was typed, which is why the import
# asked at all. Answering TheSame says it is this payment, so it is
# cleared and stamped, and the next import of the same file knows it
# outright rather than asking again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name          | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F003  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
When the possible duplicate is answered : PossibleResolution Vertical
| FitId  | F003    |
| Answer | TheSame |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Cleared | F003  |
# No transaction was created. T1 now carries F003 on the transaction in the account
# the statement was for, so reading the same file again reports a duplicate and
# asks nothing. The other side carries none: the bank has never heard of the
# category.

Attributes PossibleResolution
Description Which offered row is being answered, and what the answer is.
| Attribute | Type  | Default |
| FitId     | FitId |         |
| Answer    | Text  | TheSame |

Scenario Answering that a possible duplicate is a different transaction
Description Two genuine visits to one shop on one day for one amount. One was
# typed, and the bank is describing the other, which the book does
# not have.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name          | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F003  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
When the possible duplicate is answered : PossibleResolution Vertical
| FitId  | F003      |
| Answer | Different |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries     | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | HARRIS TEETER | Expenses:Uncategorized | -88.20 | Cleared   | F003  |
# The second is created like any new row, cleared because it came from the bank,
# and its category unknown until a payee rule or the user says otherwise. T1 is
# left exactly as it was typed, uncleared and unstamped: answering Different
# says the download is not it, which is not a statement about T1 at all. F003 is
# on the new one only, so a later file naming F003 is a duplicate of the second
# visit and nothing else.

Scenario An unanswered possible duplicate changes nothing and is asked again
Description Which is what makes it safe to put an import down and come back to
# it. Nothing is created, nothing is stamped and nothing is cleared,
# so reading the file again reaches the same question.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name          | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2026-02-05 | -88.20 | F003  | HARRIS TEETER | none | none     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| Possible | 1 |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   | FitId |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -88.20 | Uncleared | none  |
# Unchanged, and F003 is nowhere, so the same file asks the same question. An
# import that was not answered is not a decision.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two downloaded transactions that are one transfer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Importing a file for each account brings in both halves of a
# transfer as two separate transactions. Recognising them as one is a
# different problem from the matching above, because neither half is
# already in the book: both are new in the same import.
Details
# It is never done without asking. Two accounts at one bank moving the
# same round number on the same day is exactly what a genuine pair of
# separate payments looks like, and merging those would lose one of them.
# So a pair is proposed and the user approves it, and an approval can be
# remembered so that the next month's import proposes the same thing with
# the reasoning already made.

DataType TransferCandidateState
Description How far a proposed pairing has got.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                                                       |
| Proposed  | Found, and waiting to be approved or rejected               |
| Approved  | Made into one transfer                                      |
| Rejected  | Left as two transactions, each with an Uncategorized side   |
| Automatic | Approved without asking, by a remembered rule               |

Attributes TransferCandidate
Description One proposed pairing, with everything the user needs to judge it.
| Attribute   | Type                   | Default    |
| FromAccount | AccountPath            |            |
| FromDate    | Date                   | 2026-01-01 |
| FromName    | Text                   | none       |
| FromFitId   | FitId                  | none       |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath            |            |
| ToDate      | Date                   | 2026-01-01 |
| ToName      | Text                   | none       |
| ToFitId     | FitId                  | none       |
| Amount      | Money                  | 0.00       |
| DaysApart   | Integer                | 0          |
| State       | TransferCandidateState | Proposed   |

$TBD
BusinessRule When two new transactions are proposed as one transfer
Description All of these, and then it is proposed rather than done.
Details The window is two days, wider than the same day a download is
# allowed to reach when it is matched against a transaction already in
# the book. The two rules are answering different questions. There the
# evidence is an amount and a date, and a date somebody typed is the day
# they paid rather than the day the bank posted; here both halves are
# electronic and both dates are the bank's own, so two days is the lag
# between two postings rather than a guess about one. Even so every
# extra day of window is another chance to pair two payments that were
# never one transfer.
# Borne out by the three files in testdata: of the seven pairs in them,
# five are on the same day and the widest is the credit card autopay of
# 3 August, which posted to the card on the 1st. Nothing is further
# apart than two days.
Examples: TransferCandidateRule
| Test | Condition                                                            |
| 1    | Neither has been claimed by this import already                       |
| 2    | They are in two different accounts, and both accounts are real        |
| 3    | The amounts are equal and opposite                                    |
| 4    | The dates are within two days                                         |
| 5    | Neither has already been proposed as half of another pair             |

BusinessRule A payment is completed by whichever import arrives second
Description A payment from a bank account to a credit card is downloaded twice,
# once from each side, and the two downloads usually happen on
# different days in either order. Whichever arrives first records the
# payment with its other side Unassigned. Whichever arrives second
# finds that transaction and completes it, rather than recording the
# same payment again.
Details So the two halves of a transfer do not have to arrive together, which
# the first version of this rule required and which no real month looks
# like. The side that arrives second brings two things: the account, which
# fills in the first side's Unassigned Destination, and a row of its
# own -- a transaction is one account's own download, so the second half
# is a transaction in its own right, not a rewrite of the first, and it
# carries its own identifier rather than a second one squeezed onto the
# first's.
#
# Each side's payee is whatever its own file called it. A bank calls it
# a payment to the card; a card calls it a payment received. The first
# side's own payee is never touched by the second arriving -- there is
# nothing to overwrite, since each is its own transaction.
#
# Matching needs the amount equal and opposite and the dates within the
# transfer window. What it must not do is search every account in the
# book: two cards paid in the same week for similar amounts would pair
# with each other's payments. The payment payee is what narrows it.
Examples: PaymentCompletion
| WhichArrivesFirst | WhatItRecords                         | WhatTheSecondDoes              |
| The bank          | The payment, other side Unassigned    | Fills in the card, stamps its id |
| The card          | The payment, other side Unassigned    | Fills in the bank, stamps its id |
| Only one ever     | The payment, other side Unassigned    | Nothing; it stays to be assigned |

Attributes PaymentCompletion
| Attribute         | Type   | Default |
| WhichArrivesFirst | String |         |
| WhatItRecords     | String |         |
| WhatTheSecondDoes | String |         |

BusinessRule The payment payee says which accounts a payment could belong to
Description A credit card is paid by a transfer that appears on the bank
# statement as a payee -- CHASEBANK, say. Recording that payee against
# the card account is what lets a payment on the bank side find the
# card side without searching the whole book.
Details One payee can belong to several accounts, because several cards are paid
# from one bank and all of them say CHASEBANK. So the payee narrows the
# search to the accounts that carry it, and among those the amount and the
# date decide. Two cards from one bank paid different amounts in the same
# week are told apart by the amount; paid the same amount in the same week
# they are not, and that is asked rather than guessed.
#
# Where the payee matches nothing, there is no narrowing and no match: the
# payment is recorded with its other side Unassigned and waits for a
# person. That is the honest outcome -- a payment whose destination nobody
# can work out is not improved by being attached to the wrong card.
Examples: PaymentPayeeNarrowing
| TheyPayeeMatches        | ThenTheSearchIs                                  |
| One account             | That account, on amount and date                  |
| Several accounts        | Each of them, on amount and date                  |
| Several, and all match  | Asked, because only a person knows which          |
| No account              | Not searched; the payment stays Unassigned        |

Attributes PaymentPayeeNarrowing
| Attribute        | Type   | Default |
| TheyPayeeMatches | String |         |
| ThenTheSearchIs  | String |         |

Scenario A card payment downloaded from the bank waits with its other side unassigned
Description The bank is read first. It knows the money left and it knows the payee,
# and it does not know which card was paid, because the card has not
# been read yet.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder | PaymentPayee |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       | none         |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       | CHASEBANK    |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       | none         |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId  | Name      | Memo | CheckNum |
| XFER    | 2026-04-10 | -1200.00 | BNK-77 | CHASEBANK | none | none     |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee     | Destination | Amount   | Cleared | FitId  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned  | -1200.00 | Cleared | BNK-77 |
# Unassigned rather than Uncategorized: this is not a question about which
# expense it was, it is a question about which account the money went to.

Scenario The card import completes the payment the bank import recorded
Description The card is read a week later. It finds the payment already there with
# its other side unassigned, and completes it rather than recording the
# same payment again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder | PaymentPayee |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       | none         |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       | CHASEBANK    |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       | none         |
Given transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee     | Destination | Amount   | Cleared | FitId  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned  | -1200.00 | Cleared | BNK-77 |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount  | FitId  | Name             | Memo | CheckNum |
| XFER    | 2026-04-11 | 1200.00 | CC-901 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | none | none     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New     | 0 |
| Matched | 1 |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount          | Date       | Payee            | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared | FitId  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking        | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Liabilities:Chase Visa | -1200.00 | Cleared | BNK-77 |
| T2  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 2026-04-11 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Assets:Checking        | 1200.00  | Cleared | CC-901 |
# Two transactions, not one: a transaction is one account's own download, so
# completing a payment adds the card's own row rather than rewriting the
# bank's. T1's Destination is filled in with Liabilities:Chase Visa and T2 is
# new, naming T1's account back -- each keeps its own payee, the word the
# account it is in actually heard, and its own identifier, which is why an
# identifier belongs to a transaction now rather than to a transaction.

Scenario Two cards from one bank are told apart by the amount
Description Both cards say CHASEBANK, so the payee narrows the search to both and
# the amount decides between them.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type       | Placeholder | PaymentPayee |
| Assets:Checking         | Checking   | false       | none         |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa  | CreditCard | false       | CHASEBANK    |
| Liabilities:Chase Amazon | CreditCard | false      | CHASEBANK    |
| Unassigned              | OtherAsset | false       | none         |
Given transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee     | Destination | Amount   | Cleared | FitId  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned  | -1200.00 | Cleared | BNK-77 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned  | -340.00  | Cleared | BNK-78 |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Liabilities:Chase Amazon |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId  | Name             | Memo | CheckNum |
| XFER    | 2026-04-11 | 340.00 | CC-902 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | none | none     |
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New     | 0 |
| Matched | 1 |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount            | Date       | Payee            | Destination              | Amount   | Cleared | FitId  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking          | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Unassigned               | -1200.00 | Cleared | BNK-77 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking          | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Liabilities:Chase Amazon | -340.00  | Cleared | BNK-78 |
| T3  | Liabilities:Chase Amazon | 2026-04-11 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Assets:Checking          | 340.00   | Cleared | CC-902 |
# The twelve hundred is still waiting for the Visa file. Nothing about reading
# the Amazon card tells us anything about it. T3 is the new row this import
# added -- the card's own side of the payment it completed.

Scenario A row whose payee matches no account is money leaving the book
Description There is no account for it to be a transfer to, so it is not a
# transfer and nothing is waiting: the other side is a category, like
# any other payment to anyone the book does not keep.
#
# This is the common case and not a failure. Most of what leaves a
# current account goes to somebody outside the book, and a payee that
# matches no PaymentPayee is how that is recognised.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder | PaymentPayee |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       | none         |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       | CHASEBANK    |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense    | false       | none         |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       | none         |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount  | FitId  | Name          | Memo | CheckNum |
| XFER    | 2026-04-10 | -500.00 | BNK-79 | SOME OTHER CO | none | none     |
Then transactions are : ImportedTransaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared | FitId  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | SOME OTHER CO | Expenses:Uncategorized | -500.00 | Cleared | BNK-79 |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Unassigned is empty, because nothing is waiting. An XFER type in the file does
# not make something a transfer: what makes it one is both halves being accounts
# this book keeps, and that is decided by the payee, not by the type.


$TBD
BusinessRule The date windows and why they differ
Description Written down together because having four windows in one program
# invites someone to unify them, and unifying them breaks one case or
# another. Each row is a different question about two dates, and the
# window follows from whose dates they are.
Details Nearly all of this is same-day, and deliberately so. While a row is
# being identified the only window is the check-number one, where a
# number says the two records are of one payment and the days between
# them are the bank's lag and nothing else.
#
# Everywhere else inside the import the dates agree or they do not. Two
# records of one posting from one bank agree about the day, so for an
# already-imported transaction a day's difference is a difference and
# not a lag. For a typed one the evidence is an amount and a date, which
# is too little to reach across days on, so the import asks instead of
# reaching: same day offers, anything further is a new transaction.
#
# The last row is the window that used to be inside the import and is
# not any more. Pairing a transaction in one register against one in
# another is asked of transactions after the import has been accepted,
# never of rows while each one is being judged, and it has a window
# because its two dates come from two different places.
Examples: DateWindow
| Rule                                              | Window   | Why                                        |
| A download against a typed check                  | 1 day    | The number carries it; see the check rule  |
| A download against a transaction typed by hand    | Same day | An amount and a date cannot reach further; it asks |
| A download against a transaction already imported | Same day | Both dates are the bank's own posting date |
| Two new downloads proposed as one transfer        | 2 days   | Both halves are electronic and near        |
| One register's transaction against another's      | 5 days   | Asked after the import, of two books' own dates |

Attributes DateWindow
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Rule      | String |         |
| Window    | String |         |
| Why       | String |         |

Attributes TransferCandidateRule
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Test      | Integer | 0       |
| Condition | String  |         |

Scenario A transfer between two checking accounts is proposed and approved
Description The pair observed in the two checking files in testdata: the same
# amount out of one account and into the other on the same day.
